WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.069
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.069 --> 00:00:04.360
TIM HOCKIN: Welcome, everybody.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:05.484
Thank you for coming today.

00:00:05.484 --> 00:00:07.650
Glad to see a nearly full room.

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:11.130
My name is Tim, I'm one of the
tech leads on the Kubernetes

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:11.874
project.

00:00:11.874 --> 00:00:13.290
MIKE RUBIN: My
name is Mike Rubin,

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:15.729
I'm a tech lead and
manager in Kubernetes also.

00:00:15.729 --> 00:00:17.520
TIM HOCKIN: We're here
today to talk to you

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:22.700
about networking in Kubernetes,
specifically in Google Cloud.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:25.210
I hope that by the
end of today, you'll

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:26.990
all realize that
this is not nearly as

00:00:26.990 --> 00:00:30.770
scary or complicated as it
sounds, or maybe I'm wrong.

00:00:33.410 --> 00:00:34.410
So let's start.

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.750
The point of Kubernetes
is to run clusters, right?

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:41.110
A cluster is natively,
a networked construct.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:42.700
It is fundamentally
about machines

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:43.930
that talk to each other.

00:00:43.930 --> 00:00:47.491
Because of that, we have to
take networking really seriously

00:00:47.491 --> 00:00:48.490
from the very beginning.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:50.130
You can't think about--

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:52.330
you can't comprehend
Kubernetes, what we're doing,

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:56.060
without really
understanding the network.

00:00:56.060 --> 00:00:57.800
It's really easy
to get overwhelmed

00:00:57.800 --> 00:00:59.050
when we talk about Kubernetes.

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:00.490
There's a lot of new terms.

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:03.490
Everybody here is probably
familiar with basic TCIP

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:04.290
networking.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:05.920
We're going to build
on that knowledge,

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:07.295
but there's a lot
of new concepts

00:01:07.295 --> 00:01:08.336
that we need to bring in.

00:01:08.336 --> 00:01:10.390
Things like name spaces,
and virtual interfaces,

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.400
and forwarding, and IP tables.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:16.930
We are going to demystify
that today for you, I hope.

00:01:16.930 --> 00:01:19.240
So we don't really
have a lot of time

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:21.760
to do a Kubernetes 101 today.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.922
So if you want to talk
about how Kubernetes works

00:01:24.922 --> 00:01:27.130
and the basic constructs,
I'm happy to talk to people

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:31.210
afterwards, or you can find us
on GitHub, talk to me on email.

00:01:31.210 --> 00:01:33.700
But I do want to run through
a couple of background topics

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:35.824
that we're going to touch
on today during our talk,

00:01:35.824 --> 00:01:38.257
because I think it's important
that everybody understand

00:01:38.257 --> 00:01:40.090
these basics when we
talk about these words.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:44.740
So the first term I'm going to
introduce is our API server.

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:47.530
Kubernetes is a very
API centric system.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:50.080
The API server is the
center of our universe.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.990
Everything in our system
talks to the API server.

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:54.880
Our API a REST API.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.190
The R in REST is
for resource, so we

00:01:57.190 --> 00:01:59.890
tend to talk about
our API concepts

00:01:59.890 --> 00:02:02.779
in terms of
resources or objects.

00:02:02.779 --> 00:02:03.820
They mean the same thing.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.498
It's really just a
serialized blob of data

00:02:06.498 --> 00:02:08.289
that we store on our
server that represents

00:02:08.289 --> 00:02:10.150
a concept in our system.

00:02:10.150 --> 00:02:14.610
So API server is at the
center of everything.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.610
The second concept I want
to introduce is a pod.

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:18.170
Hopefully, if you
know Kubernetes,

00:02:18.170 --> 00:02:19.650
you know what a pod is.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:23.447
It is a very little,
tiny VM like thing,

00:02:23.447 --> 00:02:24.530
that runs your containers.

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:26.960
It is a group of containers
that we schedule together.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.390
It is the atom of Kubernetes.

00:02:29.390 --> 00:02:32.810
When the containers in
a pod start and stop,

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:34.220
the pods still exists.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:38.000
When you delete the pod, all
the containers go away together.

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.460
The interesting part here is
one of the fundamental pieces

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:42.470
of a pod is the network.

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:45.440
Everything inside of a
pod shares a network.

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.900
They all see each
other as local hosts,

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:51.560
they are the same
IP address, right.

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:55.410
This is really important.

00:02:55.410 --> 00:02:58.020
The next concept to
introduce, controller.

00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:01.260
So a controller is fundamentally
how Kubernetes is built.

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:03.090
It is basically
just a piece of code

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:06.690
that runs in a loop that
watches our API server

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:08.820
and responds to changes, right.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:10.756
So, for example,
when you create a pod

00:03:10.756 --> 00:03:12.630
you want to schedule
something in our system.

00:03:12.630 --> 00:03:13.920
The scheduler wakes up.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:15.620
The scheduler is a controller.

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:17.370
The scheduler makes a
decision about where

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:19.260
its going to put the
pod, it writes something

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:20.890
back the API server.

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:22.110
The cubelet wakes up.

00:03:22.110 --> 00:03:24.420
The cubelet is a controller.

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:26.810
These are a fundamental
concept that we

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:30.030
are going to build on over
and over throughout this talk.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:33.120
The main job of a
controller is to make

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:35.202
the reality match the intent.

00:03:35.202 --> 00:03:36.660
You declare what
you want it to do,

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:38.118
and we are going
to make it happen.

00:03:40.900 --> 00:03:43.390
So, a label.

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:46.180
Our API is full of these
resources, these objects.

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.220
A label is a tiny little
piece of metadata,

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:50.500
string to string
mapping key value,

00:03:50.500 --> 00:03:53.186
that you can attach any
one of our API servers.

00:03:53.186 --> 00:03:55.060
And you can attach any
number of these things

00:03:55.060 --> 00:03:57.550
to our API objects.

00:03:57.550 --> 00:04:00.129
These are used for
you to group things

00:04:00.129 --> 00:04:01.420
however you want to group them.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:03.790
We cannot possibly predict
how people are going to want

00:04:03.790 --> 00:04:07.802
to use, to group, to
slice and dice our API,

00:04:07.802 --> 00:04:09.760
so we provided a really
generic mechanism here.

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:12.700
This is fundamentally the
only grouping construct

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:13.577
in Kubernetes.

00:04:13.577 --> 00:04:15.910
We're going to build on this
when we talk about services

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:19.010
and when we talk
about endpoints.

00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:21.740
Similar to labels is
something called annotations.

00:04:21.740 --> 00:04:23.600
An annotation is
the same structure,

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:27.470
but unlike a label, which we
tend to use for identification,

00:04:27.470 --> 00:04:30.260
we tend to use annotation
for third party pieces

00:04:30.260 --> 00:04:33.230
to carry extra information
about an object.

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:37.120
So the counterpart to
labels is selectors.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:38.900
A label declares
what something is,

00:04:38.900 --> 00:04:41.780
a selector declares how you're
going to use the thing, right.

00:04:41.780 --> 00:04:43.280
So if you're a SQL
head, think of it

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.090
as select pods where
some condition.

00:04:47.090 --> 00:04:48.680
This gives us a
really loose coupling

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:50.500
between concepts in our system.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:52.700
It means that you, as a
user, can manage groups

00:04:52.700 --> 00:04:55.700
however you want to manage
them, however you want to slice

00:04:55.700 --> 00:04:56.940
and dice them.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:59.840
For example, things that
used selectors, or services,

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:02.930
deployments, replica sets.

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:05.450
So a quick example
of a selector.

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:08.030
So imagine I've got my
application here running.

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:09.710
I've got-- they're
all part of my store,

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:11.390
but I've got some front ends,
and I've got some back ends,

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:12.973
and I've got some
production services,

00:05:12.973 --> 00:05:16.190
and I've got some test services.

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:18.710
So I can select, with a
very simple selector, all

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:21.230
of my store pods that are
a part of the front end,

00:05:21.230 --> 00:05:23.330
but I can also select
all my store pods

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:25.580
that are part of
my testing, right.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:27.894
So you can see here how I
can select it anyway I want.

00:05:27.894 --> 00:05:29.810
Now I could add another
label, and I could add

00:05:29.810 --> 00:05:31.340
another dimension of selection.

00:05:31.340 --> 00:05:33.290
And you can do this
arbitrarily, however

00:05:33.290 --> 00:05:37.697
you need to do to manage
your applications.

00:05:37.697 --> 00:05:39.780
So similarly, I could just
select the whole thing.

00:05:42.740 --> 00:05:46.010
MIKE RUBIN: So let's talk
about the IP-per-pod model.

00:05:46.010 --> 00:05:48.500
Tim said earlier, that
every pod is going

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:50.310
to have its own IP address.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:52.310
There's a lot of different
ways to do networking

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:54.470
in-- with respect to containers.

00:05:54.470 --> 00:05:56.750
Docker chooses to
use-- we don't choose

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:57.930
to use a lot of complexity.

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:00.980
We want to rely in Kubernetes
on a lot of old concepts

00:06:00.980 --> 00:06:03.980
and traditional ideas involving
routing and networking.

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:07.220
There's no machine private
IPs, no port mapping.

00:06:07.220 --> 00:06:08.870
Every pod has an
IP address and it's

00:06:08.870 --> 00:06:10.600
accessible to all
the other pods,

00:06:10.600 --> 00:06:12.980
just like every virtual
machine or real machine

00:06:12.980 --> 00:06:14.720
has an IP address
and it's easiest

00:06:14.720 --> 00:06:17.750
when it's accessible to all
of the other network entities

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:20.314
that you've got in your network.

00:06:20.314 --> 00:06:21.980
We're going to be
using-- in Kubernetes,

00:06:21.980 --> 00:06:24.396
we're going to show you how
this works, Linux network name

00:06:24.396 --> 00:06:26.360
spaces, and virtual
interfaces in order

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:28.780
to implement the schema.

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:32.050
So here's a VM,
represented with a box.

00:06:32.050 --> 00:06:34.870
Most computer
diagrams are boxes,

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:36.790
when you see another box.

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:39.540
The VM is going to have
a ethernet device, eth0,

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:42.040
and this is normally how you
think of network traffic coming

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:45.100
in and out of your machine.

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:49.110
Now, in truth, there's this root
namespace, network namespace,

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:52.980
that we associate with the
ethernet device for Linux,

00:06:52.980 --> 00:06:56.370
and this has settings
for TCP/IP stack, perhaps

00:06:56.370 --> 00:06:59.310
other data structures associated
with the Linux kernel that

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:03.422
are the context and environment
for your network environment

00:07:03.422 --> 00:07:04.380
on the virtual machine.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:09.770
What we want to do is have a
pod with its own environment.

00:07:09.770 --> 00:07:11.390
We give it the
illusion that it's

00:07:11.390 --> 00:07:15.170
living inside its own space, and
that it's on its own machine.

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:17.690
So we create a network
namespace for that pod.

00:07:17.690 --> 00:07:20.690
The first pod, so we're going to
label it the really imaginative

00:07:20.690 --> 00:07:22.700
name pod1.

00:07:22.700 --> 00:07:25.460
Now we have a virtual
machine with two environments

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:27.140
that you can run
your application in,

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:30.580
each one believing that they're
inside their own network

00:07:30.580 --> 00:07:31.910
namespace.

00:07:31.910 --> 00:07:34.370
Problem here is networks are
useful when they can talk

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:38.166
to each other, right
now, these can't.

00:07:38.166 --> 00:07:40.290
Here we can bring up the
idea of a virtual ethernet

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:41.910
device for Linux.

00:07:41.910 --> 00:07:45.300
It's really just a pipe pair,
data will come in one side

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:46.870
and come out the other.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:49.450
We want these two namespaces
to talk to each other.

00:07:49.450 --> 00:07:53.220
So we take the pipe pair, and
using again some basic Linux

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:56.700
tools, we can have the pipe
pair span the namespaces.

00:07:56.700 --> 00:07:59.225
Inside the root namespace, we
can give it any name we want,

00:07:59.225 --> 00:08:02.160
and we'll call it virtual
ethernet device xx.

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:04.380
And inside the pod1,
we give it zero

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:08.240
so it looks like it's inside
its own virtual machine.

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:10.260
We can do this as
many times as we want.

00:08:10.260 --> 00:08:13.430
Here we have two pods on the
same virtual machine, each one

00:08:13.430 --> 00:08:17.060
believing that they're running
inside their own context

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:20.480
and they're talking
to the root namespace.

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:22.650
This is a step forward.

00:08:22.650 --> 00:08:26.290
But we really want them
to talk to each other.

00:08:26.290 --> 00:08:30.580
So we introduce the
concept of a bridge.

00:08:30.580 --> 00:08:32.610
The bridge is, again,
something that you've

00:08:32.610 --> 00:08:36.780
been using in networking for
decades, L2 level routing,

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:38.610
generally using
the ARP protocol.

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:40.650
I hope that most of you
are familiar with this,

00:08:40.650 --> 00:08:42.057
it's pretty old, standard stuff.

00:08:42.057 --> 00:08:43.890
You can Google it and
find out lots of tools

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:46.305
associated with it today.

00:08:46.305 --> 00:08:48.910
So let's take a look now
at traffic moving from pod

00:08:48.910 --> 00:08:52.580
to pod inside the same node.

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:56.840
Pod1 wants to send a message to
pod2, so it has an IP packet.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:01.430
Source IP is pod1 and
it's going to pod2.

00:09:01.430 --> 00:09:03.750
It leaves the pod1's
network namespace,

00:09:03.750 --> 00:09:07.550
goes through the pipe pair, hits
the virtual ethernet device,

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:10.250
gets into the root namespace,
then goes to the bridge,

00:09:10.250 --> 00:09:12.710
uses all the ARP
logic and technology

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:17.540
to find the right virtual
ethernet destination.

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:20.000
And then, once it gets to
the top of its pipe pair,

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:21.860
goes right through the
pipe pair and ends up

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:23.540
in pod2's namespace.

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:25.700
It's pretty straightforward,
pretty simple,

00:09:25.700 --> 00:09:28.010
and using basic
concepts that, if you're

00:09:28.010 --> 00:09:30.080
familiar with just
rudimentary networking,

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:33.110
should feel familiar to you.

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:36.020
So pods have to b e reachable
across VMs also, not just

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:37.550
inside of one VM.

00:09:37.550 --> 00:09:40.040
Kubernetes itself doesn't
care how you do this.

00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:44.180
You can use L2, L3, overlay,
carrying pigeons, unicorns,

00:09:44.180 --> 00:09:47.060
[AUDIO OUT] matter, but there
has to be some standard way

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:48.830
to do this stuff.

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:51.830
Every VM is given an
IP block, and every pod

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:54.320
is given an IP address
inside that block.

00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:57.590
So one thing we have to do
for Google Container Engine

00:09:57.590 --> 00:10:01.040
is teach the network how to
route packets in-between VMs.

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:04.310
So, luckily, we have Tim here,
he knows all about this stuff,

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:06.301
and he's going to show us.

00:10:06.301 --> 00:10:07.800
TIM HOCKIN: So
building on the image

00:10:07.800 --> 00:10:10.920
that we looked at before, these
are the same sorts of machines,

00:10:10.920 --> 00:10:12.669
only now we've got two
of them and they're

00:10:12.669 --> 00:10:13.780
connected to our network.

00:10:13.780 --> 00:10:16.232
So as Michael mentioned, we
have to teach the network

00:10:16.232 --> 00:10:17.190
how to do this routing.

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:19.710
So what happens when
we send a packet out

00:10:19.710 --> 00:10:22.150
of pod1 destined for pod4?

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:24.890
All right, same as before,
it's going to leave the pod,

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:26.750
it's going to enter
the root namespace,

00:10:26.750 --> 00:10:28.000
we're going to hit the bridge.

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:30.630
Now, the ARP is going
to fail on that bridge.

00:10:30.630 --> 00:10:32.220
There's nobody on
that bridge who's

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:33.660
answering this IP
address, so it's

00:10:33.660 --> 00:10:35.284
going to send it out
the default route.

00:10:35.284 --> 00:10:37.680
The default route, in
this case, is eth0.

00:10:37.680 --> 00:10:40.140
So we send it out the default
route, it leaves the VM,

00:10:40.140 --> 00:10:43.220
and it enters Google
Cloud's network.

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:45.647
And it promptly goes nowhere.

00:10:45.647 --> 00:10:48.230
It goes nowhere because we have
anti-spoofing protection built

00:10:48.230 --> 00:10:49.940
into Google Cloud,
and the network

00:10:49.940 --> 00:10:51.920
knows that this VM
has an IP address

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:54.570
and the source IP is not
the right IP address.

00:10:54.570 --> 00:10:57.680
So we have to set up
a couple of things

00:10:57.680 --> 00:10:59.310
before we can make
any this work.

00:10:59.310 --> 00:11:01.940
So the first thing we do,
when we create a VM, we tell

00:11:01.940 --> 00:11:04.100
GCP that these are
all routers, right.

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:07.040
And the routers have a flag
called --can-ip-forward,

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:10.160
which means that it's allowed to
disable anti-spoof protection,

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:11.300
but for only for this VM.

00:11:11.300 --> 00:11:14.000
The rest of the network
is still spoof safe.

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:18.510
Then we add a single static
route per VM, which says,

00:11:18.510 --> 00:11:21.290
hey, if you see any packets
bound for this IP range,

00:11:21.290 --> 00:11:24.290
send it over to the VM, and
the VM will know what to do.

00:11:24.290 --> 00:11:24.820
Right.

00:11:24.820 --> 00:11:27.830
The GCP network
handles the rest.

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:30.640
So let's go back here, we've
got this packet, it's from pod1,

00:11:30.640 --> 00:11:32.560
it's going to pod4, right.

00:11:32.560 --> 00:11:35.000
This time it passes through
the network successfully,

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:37.840
and it's delivered
to the second VM,

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:40.030
and the packet flows
in the exact same way

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:41.120
that we looked at before.

00:11:41.120 --> 00:11:42.661
There's nothing
special here, there's

00:11:42.661 --> 00:11:45.160
no translations required,
there's no encapsulation,

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:47.800
there's no overlays,
it just flows

00:11:47.800 --> 00:11:50.261
the way a network should flow.

00:11:50.261 --> 00:11:52.760
MIKE RUBIN: So one other thing
that's important to remember,

00:11:52.760 --> 00:11:54.860
we just talked about
network namespace set up,

00:11:54.860 --> 00:11:56.600
and virtual ethernet
device set up,

00:11:56.600 --> 00:12:00.130
and all of these areas in
GCloud to turn off spoofing.

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:01.790
It's all automatically
handled for you

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:03.290
in Google Container Engine.

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:04.970
You don't have to
go to the man pages

00:12:04.970 --> 00:12:07.970
and learn all of these pieces,
you just turn on the system

00:12:07.970 --> 00:12:12.010
and by default, as you start
creating pods and creating--

00:12:12.010 --> 00:12:13.950
these things will
just be set up.

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:16.490
But let's get back
to progressing

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:19.730
through the network stack inside
of Google Container Engine.

00:12:19.730 --> 00:12:21.150
How do we deal with change?

00:12:21.150 --> 00:12:22.974
Your cluster is a
living, dynamic system.

00:12:22.974 --> 00:12:24.890
Sometimes you're going
to want to scale it up,

00:12:24.890 --> 00:12:27.620
as load increases, or are you're
going to want to scale it down,

00:12:27.620 --> 00:12:31.640
possibly, as low
decreases, or maybe revenue

00:12:31.640 --> 00:12:34.552
becomes more of an interesting
issue in your life.

00:12:34.552 --> 00:12:37.010
Rolling updates, you're going
to want to turn off some pods

00:12:37.010 --> 00:12:39.620
and then turn them on
again with new software.

00:12:39.620 --> 00:12:41.330
You're going to
want the pods, maybe

00:12:41.330 --> 00:12:44.180
if they crash or hang to be
unscheduled and rescheduled

00:12:44.180 --> 00:12:46.400
somewhere else, maybe
the VM is rebooting.

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:49.910
All of these events involve your
pod getting a new IP address,

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:52.150
and keeping track of all
of these IP addresses,

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:53.120
it's a lot of work.

00:12:53.120 --> 00:12:54.710
You know, I have to start
keeping track of state,

00:12:54.710 --> 00:12:56.240
and anyone who's
kept track of state

00:12:56.240 --> 00:12:57.800
knows that it's never fun.

00:12:57.800 --> 00:13:00.770
So you want to find a way
to have your systems network

00:13:00.770 --> 00:13:03.110
traffic be able to
find the pods that you

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:06.320
care about without a lot
of work on your behalf.

00:13:06.320 --> 00:13:09.750
Google Container Engine helps
you with this, with services.

00:13:09.750 --> 00:13:12.290
Dun dun dun!

00:13:12.290 --> 00:13:14.762
The service abstraction--
it's an abstraction

00:13:14.762 --> 00:13:16.220
and another resource
or object that

00:13:16.220 --> 00:13:18.770
lives inside the API server.

00:13:18.770 --> 00:13:22.370
It's basically a mapping
of a cluster IP address

00:13:22.370 --> 00:13:25.370
or a virtual IP address to a
group of endpoints, usually

00:13:25.370 --> 00:13:26.420
pods.

00:13:26.420 --> 00:13:28.970
It provides you a
stable VIP, and the VIP,

00:13:28.970 --> 00:13:31.790
when you send traffic to it,
will then route that traffic

00:13:31.790 --> 00:13:34.700
to the pods that are being
selectively associated

00:13:34.700 --> 00:13:36.110
with that service.

00:13:36.110 --> 00:13:38.600
We're going to examine the
default implementation of this

00:13:38.600 --> 00:13:40.370
in Google Container Engine.

00:13:40.370 --> 00:13:42.590
But like many things in
Google Container Engine,

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:45.200
you can always componentized
it and try to do something else

00:13:45.200 --> 00:13:47.500
if that's really what you need.

00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:50.482
The set of the pods behind a
service can change dynamically,

00:13:50.482 --> 00:13:52.940
and it doesn't really matter,
as long as you have that VIP,

00:13:52.940 --> 00:13:56.380
your traffic can
reach those pods.

00:13:56.380 --> 00:13:58.080
So let's try to
create a service.

00:13:58.080 --> 00:13:59.920
Here's the yaml.

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:02.810
The fields that really matter
is the name, in this case,

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:05.170
we're going to call it
the store-be for maybe all

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:06.640
the store backend pods.

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:10.390
And this selector here says
application that matches store,

00:14:10.390 --> 00:14:12.460
and the roles that
are the backend.

00:14:12.460 --> 00:14:14.020
So the selector
field here is going

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:16.300
to then find all of the
pods, that with these labels,

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:19.530
and associate them
with this service.

00:14:19.530 --> 00:14:21.330
What you get back
is some information

00:14:21.330 --> 00:14:23.202
from Kubernetes API's server.

00:14:23.202 --> 00:14:25.410
It's going to automatically
create a distributed load

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:29.640
balancer for you, and it's also
going to allocate this cluster

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:32.220
IP 10.9.3.76.

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:34.620
And this is the IP address
that within the cluster,

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:36.510
any traffic to
that IP address is

00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:39.120
going to make sure it
reaches one of those pods,

00:14:39.120 --> 00:14:41.370
and it's going to smear
this traffic over randomly

00:14:41.370 --> 00:14:42.042
and fairly.

00:14:42.042 --> 00:14:43.500
The idea here is
that it's balanced

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:46.380
across all of those pods.

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:49.230
Now I'm going to take a slight
digression, because this word

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:51.480
is a very accurate
word in this context,

00:14:51.480 --> 00:14:53.520
but it's overloaded
in networking

00:14:53.520 --> 00:14:55.240
and sometimes in Kubernetes.

00:14:55.240 --> 00:14:58.560
The idea of what is an
Endpoints resource or object

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.300
in the API server.

00:15:00.300 --> 00:15:03.480
As we said earlier, every
time you create a service,

00:15:03.480 --> 00:15:05.869
you're also having to select
the pods that are associated

00:15:05.869 --> 00:15:07.410
with that service
that the traffic is

00:15:07.410 --> 00:15:09.210
going to be routed
to, and you have

00:15:09.210 --> 00:15:10.590
a selector associated with it.

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:13.470
Well, when you create a service
in Google Container Engine,

00:15:13.470 --> 00:15:16.590
you also have Google
Container Engine for you

00:15:16.590 --> 00:15:20.537
create an endpoints resource or
object with the same selector.

00:15:20.537 --> 00:15:22.620
So if you have a bunch of
pods that you associated

00:15:22.620 --> 00:15:26.100
with that service, in this
case, app: store role: be,

00:15:26.100 --> 00:15:29.050
well, they're going to be
found by the endpoints--

00:15:29.050 --> 00:15:31.050
the Kubernetes system,
which will associate them

00:15:31.050 --> 00:15:33.720
with the endpoints, and it's
going to automatically create

00:15:33.720 --> 00:15:37.720
for you this endpoints object.

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:39.280
And why do we need this?

00:15:39.280 --> 00:15:41.907
Well, it turns out that in
Google Container Engine,

00:15:41.907 --> 00:15:43.740
and in Kubernetes in
general and open source

00:15:43.740 --> 00:15:46.380
also, a lot of objects
and controllers

00:15:46.380 --> 00:15:49.470
really want to know about
all of the pods associated

00:15:49.470 --> 00:15:50.640
with a service.

00:15:50.640 --> 00:15:53.190
And they make this query
over and over and over again,

00:15:53.190 --> 00:15:54.780
and it's an expensive query.

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:57.360
So as an implementation
detail, we've

00:15:57.360 --> 00:15:59.550
decided in Google
Container Engine

00:15:59.550 --> 00:16:03.030
to create a resource just for
this mapping of service name

00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:05.010
to pod IP addresses.

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:07.200
This way, as pods
come and go, we

00:16:07.200 --> 00:16:09.210
have a convenient
cache of the locations

00:16:09.210 --> 00:16:12.670
and IP addresses for
all of these pods.

00:16:12.670 --> 00:16:14.770
The-- [AUDIO OUT]
makes this kind of--

00:16:14.770 --> 00:16:15.747
coming in and out--

00:16:15.747 --> 00:16:21.006
[AUDIO OUT] in, out, much
like a pod going up and down.

00:16:21.006 --> 00:16:22.880
Only thing that makes
this really interesting

00:16:22.880 --> 00:16:25.910
and sometimes confusing is
it has the name Endpoints,

00:16:25.910 --> 00:16:29.000
and we have HTTP Endpoints, and
we have a lot of other network

00:16:29.000 --> 00:16:32.210
terms that refer to endpoints.

00:16:32.210 --> 00:16:34.310
That's the only reason
we brought it up today.

00:16:34.310 --> 00:16:35.030
It confused me.

00:16:35.030 --> 00:16:36.170
It took me like two
months to realize

00:16:36.170 --> 00:16:37.794
exactly what we were
talking about when

00:16:37.794 --> 00:16:40.675
we started with the project.

00:16:40.675 --> 00:16:42.050
Let's take a look
now at the life

00:16:42.050 --> 00:16:45.170
of a packet from the
pod to a service.

00:16:45.170 --> 00:16:47.505
Looks very familiar to
what we've seen before.

00:16:47.505 --> 00:16:49.520
It's a pod, and--

00:16:49.520 --> 00:16:52.080
should I move, or is this good?

00:16:52.080 --> 00:16:52.610
OK.

00:16:52.610 --> 00:16:53.764
The packet leaves the pod.

00:16:53.764 --> 00:16:55.430
Source IP address is
the pod, and what's

00:16:55.430 --> 00:16:57.650
different now is the
destination IP address is

00:16:57.650 --> 00:17:00.080
going to the service,
in this case, service 1.

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:04.160
Goes from the network namespace
to the pipe, hits the bridge,

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:07.280
but instead of going
to another pod,

00:17:07.280 --> 00:17:11.180
it's going to instead
go to IP tables.

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:14.339
In IP tables, we're
going to do a DNAT.

00:17:14.339 --> 00:17:16.720
And the DNAT's going to take
that destination service,

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:21.260
and remember, service is just
a handle to a group of pods.

00:17:21.260 --> 00:17:23.990
It's going to take that service,
and pick the pod that we

00:17:23.990 --> 00:17:25.780
want this packet to go to.

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:28.790
In this case, pod99, and
it's going to use a system

00:17:28.790 --> 00:17:33.750
called Conntrack to remember
which pod it sent this IP--

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:37.040
this connection of
traffic to Conntrack.

00:17:37.040 --> 00:17:39.330
Conntrack is
another Linux system

00:17:39.330 --> 00:17:41.190
that does connection
tracking for you.

00:17:41.190 --> 00:17:44.040
It bases it on the
5-tuple of IP header

00:17:44.040 --> 00:17:45.810
that you're sending
the traffic out to,

00:17:45.810 --> 00:17:47.434
and it does a lot of
other things, too,

00:17:47.434 --> 00:17:49.350
but it's going to remember
where it came from

00:17:49.350 --> 00:17:51.665
or where the initial
destination was.

00:17:51.665 --> 00:17:53.040
We're going to
see how it's being

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:58.650
used for Kubernetes networking
as we continue the talk.

00:17:58.650 --> 00:18:01.380
So now we have rewritten
the destination IP address

00:18:01.380 --> 00:18:05.490
for pod99, or IP tables
has done the load balancing

00:18:05.490 --> 00:18:08.250
on this machine to take
our traffic instead

00:18:08.250 --> 00:18:11.310
of to the service
IP, to the actual pod

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:15.850
that the service
is fronting for us.

00:18:15.850 --> 00:18:17.560
And then when that
pod has responded,

00:18:17.560 --> 00:18:19.840
it comes back,
coming from pod99,

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:22.654
saying I want to
go back to pod1.

00:18:22.654 --> 00:18:24.580
It hits the IP tables.

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:30.310
The IP tables uses
Conntrack [AUDIO OUT]

00:18:30.310 --> 00:18:34.240
--uses Conntrack to
[AUDIO OUT] the packet,

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:40.990
rewrites the source IP header
from pod99 to service 1.

00:18:40.990 --> 00:18:42.940
Sorry, we're having some
audio stuff going on.

00:18:42.940 --> 00:18:44.870
Is this cool?

00:18:44.870 --> 00:18:47.180
OK, I'll continue.

00:18:47.180 --> 00:18:49.070
And then it goes
on its merry way,

00:18:49.070 --> 00:18:51.110
just like we've described
a few times already,

00:18:51.110 --> 00:18:55.070
all the way back to the
pod1's network namespace.

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:58.637
So let's take a little
more look at the IP tables.

00:18:58.637 --> 00:19:00.470
The IP table rules, if
you look at them raw,

00:19:00.470 --> 00:19:02.194
are really, really
scary, but this

00:19:02.194 --> 00:19:04.610
is some of the things you
really have to remember when you

00:19:04.610 --> 00:19:07.100
look at services and IP tables.

00:19:07.100 --> 00:19:10.940
The IP tables are really
performing one piece of logic.

00:19:10.940 --> 00:19:13.700
They're trying to route
that packet and the traffic

00:19:13.700 --> 00:19:16.700
in general to the pods
behind the service.

00:19:16.700 --> 00:19:19.280
So they check to see
the destination IP

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:21.855
and the port match the
service's [AUDIO OUT] port,

00:19:21.855 --> 00:19:24.230
and if they do, they're going
to pick one of the backends

00:19:24.230 --> 00:19:26.660
at random and then
rewrite the destination

00:19:26.660 --> 00:19:30.042
and send that connection on its
way to that appropriate pod.

00:19:30.042 --> 00:19:32.000
Now here's something else
that's kind of funny,

00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:33.860
and a lot of people
have asked about.

00:19:33.860 --> 00:19:36.780
All of this work is happening
within the Linux kernel.

00:19:36.780 --> 00:19:40.700
We are not copying the data out
of the kernel into userspace,

00:19:40.700 --> 00:19:43.040
playing with the packets,
and then sending it

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:44.600
back into userspace again.

00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:47.330
It's really inefficient,
it's really not the right way

00:19:47.330 --> 00:19:50.640
to do it, but it is
the way, when we first

00:19:50.640 --> 00:19:54.560
began working on Kubernetes
in Google Container Engine,

00:19:54.560 --> 00:19:55.970
it is how things used to work.

00:19:55.970 --> 00:19:58.220
And this is just sort of
getting things off the ground

00:19:58.220 --> 00:19:59.420
and prototyping.

00:19:59.420 --> 00:20:03.260
So because of that,
the system and the pod

00:20:03.260 --> 00:20:06.470
that actually maintains
all of these IP table rules

00:20:06.470 --> 00:20:08.450
is called the cube
proxy, because it

00:20:08.450 --> 00:20:11.240
used to be a proxy for
all this network traffic.

00:20:11.240 --> 00:20:13.745
It's not actually a proxy
today, all it does is--

00:20:17.470 --> 00:20:18.680
just talk into it?

00:20:18.680 --> 00:20:20.122
Can I sing and dance?

00:20:20.122 --> 00:20:20.830
All right, great.

00:20:20.830 --> 00:20:22.246
TIM HOCKIN: I don't
know, can you?

00:20:22.246 --> 00:20:25.840
MIKE RUBIN: No, I can't
sing, nor can I dance.

00:20:25.840 --> 00:20:28.150
Thank you, Tim.

00:20:28.150 --> 00:20:30.640
TIM HOCKIN: My pleasure.

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:33.940
MIKE RUBIN: So-- cube proxy.

00:20:33.940 --> 00:20:37.690
Cube proxy is a pod that
lives on every node,

00:20:37.690 --> 00:20:39.966
and it maintains these
IP table rules for you.

00:20:39.966 --> 00:20:41.590
It used to do this
in user space, which

00:20:41.590 --> 00:20:43.630
is why it has a name
that's very confusing,

00:20:43.630 --> 00:20:46.540
but now it just maintains
the IP table rules

00:20:46.540 --> 00:20:48.106
that keep the services running.

00:20:48.106 --> 00:20:49.480
It's a controller,
just like many

00:20:49.480 --> 00:20:51.910
of the other pieces of
Kubernetes infrastructure,

00:20:51.910 --> 00:20:54.190
and it watches the API
for services changes,

00:20:54.190 --> 00:20:56.325
and when they change it's
going to update the IP

00:20:56.325 --> 00:20:56.950
tables for you.

00:21:00.870 --> 00:21:04.170
So let's go take a look at
life of a packet from pod

00:21:04.170 --> 00:21:05.760
to service.

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:09.330
Right now, the source is
going to come to the service

00:21:09.330 --> 00:21:13.110
and destination's
going to a pod.

00:21:13.110 --> 00:21:14.760
Destination is
coming to the pod--

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:15.840
oh wait, I'm in
the wrong button.

00:21:15.840 --> 00:21:16.200
TIM HOCKIN: Yeah.

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:18.033
MIKE RUBIN: Between the
mic and the singing,

00:21:18.033 --> 00:21:19.830
it's way too much fun.

00:21:19.830 --> 00:21:21.270
Services.

00:21:21.270 --> 00:21:23.070
DNS is just another
service inside

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:26.190
of Google Container Engine,
that's how we implemented it.

00:21:26.190 --> 00:21:28.840
You don't really want to
hard code the name and IP--

00:21:28.840 --> 00:21:30.840
you want to hard
code the IP address

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:33.190
of all of these
services into your code.

00:21:33.190 --> 00:21:35.010
But, if you know the
name of the service,

00:21:35.010 --> 00:21:37.051
you may want to hard code
the name of the service

00:21:37.051 --> 00:21:39.270
to direct your traffic to
all of the pods associated

00:21:39.270 --> 00:21:40.440
with that service.

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:43.770
So Google Container
Engine will automatically,

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:46.170
when you create that service,
put the service's name

00:21:46.170 --> 00:21:51.030
inside the DNS space inside
the Google Container Engine.

00:21:51.030 --> 00:21:53.174
It uses normal A
and SRV records,

00:21:53.174 --> 00:21:54.840
so you don't really
have to do anything,

00:21:54.840 --> 00:21:58.020
and DNS itself runs as
a pod and as a service

00:21:58.020 --> 00:22:00.345
for you, just using the same
infrastructure that we're

00:22:00.345 --> 00:22:02.550
asking people to use.

00:22:02.550 --> 00:22:06.060
DNS service has got a little
bit of extra magic and sugar.

00:22:06.060 --> 00:22:08.610
Just like, other services,
things that we can show you.

00:22:08.610 --> 00:22:11.070
Any service-- and we
just used this for DNS--

00:22:11.070 --> 00:22:13.440
can request a
particular cluster IP.

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:17.520
So we know what DNS, we want to
use the IP address 10.0.0.10.

00:22:17.520 --> 00:22:19.614
So we specify that when
we create the service,

00:22:19.614 --> 00:22:21.780
and we know that we're going
to get that IP address.

00:22:21.780 --> 00:22:24.000
We'll get an error when
we create the service.

00:22:24.000 --> 00:22:25.980
Also something special
about DNS is it's auto

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:27.570
scaled to the cluster size.

00:22:27.570 --> 00:22:30.480
It's actually a ratio
of nodes versus cores,

00:22:30.480 --> 00:22:32.070
but the idea here
is we don't really

00:22:32.070 --> 00:22:34.650
ever want DNS to be overloaded.

00:22:34.650 --> 00:22:37.080
Google Container Engine has
a little bit more surprise

00:22:37.080 --> 00:22:38.880
and fun and adventure
when we do that,

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:41.970
so we auto scale it to keep
things running correctly.

00:22:41.970 --> 00:22:45.510
Again, just like other services,
the [INAUDIBLE] is stable.

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:47.024
Services are simple
and powerful.

00:22:47.024 --> 00:22:48.440
You can use any
port you want, you

00:22:48.440 --> 00:22:49.939
don't have to worry
about conflicts.

00:22:49.939 --> 00:22:52.290
You can request a
particular cluster IP,

00:22:52.290 --> 00:22:54.420
as we just described,
and you can remap ports.

00:22:56.775 --> 00:22:58.150
That's all there
really is to it.

00:22:58.150 --> 00:22:59.525
The big thing to
remember, again,

00:22:59.525 --> 00:23:01.470
is services are an abstraction.

00:23:01.470 --> 00:23:06.610
They're an idea of mapping of
a name and IP address to pods

00:23:06.610 --> 00:23:09.160
that lives and you can
manage in the API server,

00:23:09.160 --> 00:23:10.900
but the implementation
of it is kind of

00:23:10.900 --> 00:23:13.570
spread across all the
IP tables and maintained

00:23:13.570 --> 00:23:17.890
by cube proxy inside of
Google Container Engine.

00:23:17.890 --> 00:23:20.080
It doesn't really have
one running process that's

00:23:20.080 --> 00:23:21.790
doing all of this
work, but all you

00:23:21.790 --> 00:23:23.890
need to know as a
customer and a client

00:23:23.890 --> 00:23:26.680
is you set up the service,
you hit the client IP address

00:23:26.680 --> 00:23:28.401
or you hit that DNS
name, and that's

00:23:28.401 --> 00:23:29.650
really all you have to handle.

00:23:29.650 --> 00:23:31.300
Google Container
Engine takes care

00:23:31.300 --> 00:23:34.770
of all this complexity for you.

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:36.580
Let's talk about sending
external traffic.

00:23:36.580 --> 00:23:39.930
What if you want to take traffic
outside of your GCP project

00:23:39.930 --> 00:23:42.770
to the outside world?

00:23:42.770 --> 00:23:45.220
Egress.

00:23:45.220 --> 00:23:50.840
VMs all get private IPs in
the 10.0.0.0/8 IP range,

00:23:50.840 --> 00:23:52.795
and VMs can also get public IPs.

00:23:52.795 --> 00:23:55.930
When they get a public IP,
they get one public IP mapped

00:23:55.930 --> 00:23:58.499
to one private IP.

00:23:58.499 --> 00:24:00.040
So let's look at
the life of a packet

00:24:00.040 --> 00:24:02.200
from going from VM
to the internet.

00:24:02.200 --> 00:24:05.290
Here we have our same
happy virtual machine,

00:24:05.290 --> 00:24:11.040
with the root namespace zero
device inside a GCP project.

00:24:11.040 --> 00:24:13.860
We send a packet out with
the VM internal's IP address.

00:24:13.860 --> 00:24:17.750
The source going to 8.8.8.8.

00:24:17.750 --> 00:24:20.540
And then in the NAT
subsystem of the GCP project,

00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:23.150
we rewrite the internal
address to the external,

00:24:23.150 --> 00:24:28.330
so the external internet knows
where this packet came from

00:24:28.330 --> 00:24:30.700
and can route it back.

00:24:30.700 --> 00:24:32.720
And away we go.

00:24:32.720 --> 00:24:36.230
And then, because 8.8.8.8 is
maintained by this fabulously

00:24:36.230 --> 00:24:38.840
happy company, comes
back really fast,

00:24:38.840 --> 00:24:41.935
and we go back to the
VM external address.

00:24:41.935 --> 00:24:44.060
Also, for those of you who
are not in the audience,

00:24:44.060 --> 00:24:48.470
there's one person laughing at
my jokes, which is really cool.

00:24:48.470 --> 00:24:50.000
We rewrite the
external IP address

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:53.139
to the internal IP address.

00:24:53.139 --> 00:24:55.680
It's not really a big deal, it's
normal routing, like always.

00:24:55.680 --> 00:24:57.190
But what happens with the pod?

00:24:57.190 --> 00:24:59.350
Everything's a little different.

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:02.980
Just like always, the pod
comes from the root namespace,

00:25:02.980 --> 00:25:05.680
comes up to the device,
hits the bridge,

00:25:05.680 --> 00:25:09.380
comes out through the VM,
the source here-- sorry,

00:25:09.380 --> 00:25:10.150
glanced over that.

00:25:10.150 --> 00:25:13.150
The source here is the pod
IP address, hits the NAT,

00:25:13.150 --> 00:25:16.270
[BUZZER NOISE], it's dropped.

00:25:16.270 --> 00:25:19.000
The one-to-one NAT inside
the GCP, for security,

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:22.540
only really understands and
allows VM IPs to get out.

00:25:22.540 --> 00:25:25.810
The pod IP it is not equal
to VM IP, so it gets dropped.

00:25:25.810 --> 00:25:27.610
When in doubt,
what's the answer?

00:25:27.610 --> 00:25:29.050
IP tables.

00:25:29.050 --> 00:25:32.200
And so here we MASKERADE
again, or use Source NAT,

00:25:32.200 --> 00:25:35.050
and we apply this to any packet
with a destination that's

00:25:35.050 --> 00:25:40.550
outside of the internal
cluster's IP range.

00:25:40.550 --> 00:25:42.674
So let's take a quick
look at that again.

00:25:42.674 --> 00:25:44.090
Life of a packet,
pod to internet,

00:25:44.090 --> 00:25:45.800
we go all the way
up to the IP tables.

00:25:45.800 --> 00:25:50.570
We rewrite the pod1 source to
the VM internal IP address,

00:25:50.570 --> 00:25:54.710
it leaves the VM, it goes
to the NAT subsystem.

00:25:54.710 --> 00:25:57.230
Now we rewrite it again,
from VM internal to external,

00:25:57.230 --> 00:25:59.100
just as before.

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:04.370
Goes to 8.8.8.8,
comes right back,

00:26:04.370 --> 00:26:06.830
we rewrite the destination,
external to internal, just

00:26:06.830 --> 00:26:10.460
as before, hits the
ethernet device,

00:26:10.460 --> 00:26:13.580
goes into the IP tables, we use
Conntrack in the same system

00:26:13.580 --> 00:26:16.670
that we built all this
logic on, to now we

00:26:16.670 --> 00:26:19.280
write it with the
pod1's IP address.

00:26:19.280 --> 00:26:22.250
And by now, I hope some of
you are tired of this path

00:26:22.250 --> 00:26:26.690
as we go back to the
pod1 network namespace.

00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:29.020
Tim?

00:26:29.020 --> 00:26:32.530
TIM HOCKIN: So that gives
you a pretty good picture

00:26:32.530 --> 00:26:36.100
of how packets move around
within your GCP cluster, right?

00:26:36.100 --> 00:26:38.020
And that was super cool,
but it's not really

00:26:38.020 --> 00:26:40.180
that interesting,
because most of us

00:26:40.180 --> 00:26:41.800
have to deal with
the outside world.

00:26:41.800 --> 00:26:44.830
And getting out to
GitHub is cool and all,

00:26:44.830 --> 00:26:47.020
but it's not going
to sell your product.

00:26:47.020 --> 00:26:49.390
You've got to take
traffic in, right?

00:26:49.390 --> 00:26:51.850
This is a really
complicated problem.

00:26:51.850 --> 00:26:55.090
For Kubernetes in
general, we have

00:26:55.090 --> 00:26:56.860
to build it to the
particular platforms

00:26:56.860 --> 00:26:57.818
that you're running on.

00:26:57.818 --> 00:27:00.040
So Kubernetes is on
GCP is going to rely

00:27:00.040 --> 00:27:02.890
on a couple of
different GCP products

00:27:02.890 --> 00:27:04.730
to bring traffic
into the cluster.

00:27:04.730 --> 00:27:06.970
And we're going to see
how we use those products.

00:27:06.970 --> 00:27:09.940
So in particular, we,
for the Kubernetes API,

00:27:09.940 --> 00:27:12.040
where we set a
type load balancer,

00:27:12.040 --> 00:27:14.950
we're going to use Google's
Network Load Balancer at L4.

00:27:14.950 --> 00:27:16.840
And where we use the
ingress resource,

00:27:16.840 --> 00:27:20.800
we're going to use the L7 load
balancer, the HTTP and HTTPS

00:27:20.800 --> 00:27:23.030
load balancer.

00:27:23.030 --> 00:27:25.160
So let's look at L4
first, because it's

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:28.190
the less exciting one.

00:27:28.190 --> 00:27:30.380
So we looked at
service earlier, right,

00:27:30.380 --> 00:27:32.150
Michael mentioned cluster IPs.

00:27:32.150 --> 00:27:34.520
So we're going to change
the type from a cluster IP

00:27:34.520 --> 00:27:35.697
to a load balancer.

00:27:35.697 --> 00:27:37.280
Now the way we've
set this up is these

00:27:37.280 --> 00:27:40.760
are sort of concentric, so
cluster IP, node port load

00:27:40.760 --> 00:27:41.260
balancer.

00:27:41.260 --> 00:27:42.886
So load bouncer still
is a cluster IP.

00:27:42.886 --> 00:27:44.510
It's still going to
get allocated an IP

00:27:44.510 --> 00:27:46.130
address within my
cluster, but what

00:27:46.130 --> 00:27:49.520
happens when I set this, is
another controller wakes up--

00:27:49.520 --> 00:27:51.690
this is our Google Cloud
controller-- it wakes up

00:27:51.690 --> 00:27:54.080
and it says, hey I know
what to do with that.

00:27:54.080 --> 00:27:56.930
I'm going to go off and I'm
going to create a Google Load

00:27:56.930 --> 00:27:59.030
Balancer, and I'm going
to set up all the routing,

00:27:59.030 --> 00:28:01.780
and I'm going to
make it work for you.

00:28:01.780 --> 00:28:03.870
So when it's done
with that, it's

00:28:03.870 --> 00:28:06.300
going write back to the service
you have a load balancer,

00:28:06.300 --> 00:28:08.790
and it's ingress point is
this IP address, right.

00:28:08.790 --> 00:28:11.550
So now you, as an end user,
you can take that IP address.

00:28:11.550 --> 00:28:13.950
You can stick it in your
DNS, you can share it

00:28:13.950 --> 00:28:16.800
with your customers, you can
write it on the bathroom wall,

00:28:16.800 --> 00:28:20.060
people will find your store.

00:28:20.060 --> 00:28:23.800
So let's look at how this
works, like, in practice.

00:28:23.800 --> 00:28:26.740
So we have a cluster here,
and to make things fun,

00:28:26.740 --> 00:28:27.970
this cluster has three VMs.

00:28:27.970 --> 00:28:30.850
One VM happens to have
one of my particular pod,

00:28:30.850 --> 00:28:35.900
one VM has none,
and one VM has two.

00:28:35.900 --> 00:28:38.397
So now I create that service
with type load balancer,

00:28:38.397 --> 00:28:39.980
this controller wakes
up, and goes off

00:28:39.980 --> 00:28:41.990
and it creates a Google
Network Load Balancer.

00:28:41.990 --> 00:28:44.780
If anybody is really familiar
with the Google Cloud API,

00:28:44.780 --> 00:28:46.850
this is a forwarding
rule, right.

00:28:46.850 --> 00:28:48.680
It's going to create
a forwarding rule,

00:28:48.680 --> 00:28:50.304
and it's going to
point that forwarding

00:28:50.304 --> 00:28:52.700
rule at all of my VMs.

00:28:52.700 --> 00:28:55.764
And this means we don't ever
have to update the forwarding

00:28:55.764 --> 00:28:57.680
rule, except if the VMs
change, because that's

00:28:57.680 --> 00:28:58.700
sort of a slow API.

00:28:58.700 --> 00:29:02.300
So we program it and
program to all of these VMs.

00:29:02.300 --> 00:29:05.090
So now, user has
found our store,

00:29:05.090 --> 00:29:06.840
and they're going to
send us some traffic.

00:29:06.840 --> 00:29:10.250
The packet here from the
client to the load balancer,

00:29:10.250 --> 00:29:12.269
this is important.

00:29:12.269 --> 00:29:13.810
Now we go through
this load balancer,

00:29:13.810 --> 00:29:15.410
and the load balancer
has to make a decision.

00:29:15.410 --> 00:29:18.070
It looks at all of the VMs
that are eligible to be chosen,

00:29:18.070 --> 00:29:20.470
it's going to pick
one of these three.

00:29:20.470 --> 00:29:22.705
In this case, we're
going to pick VM1.

00:29:22.705 --> 00:29:24.580
It doesn't really matter
which one we choose,

00:29:24.580 --> 00:29:27.550
because the same flow is going
to happen in all of them.

00:29:27.550 --> 00:29:30.800
So now we send a packet on to--

00:29:30.800 --> 00:29:32.494
shoot, we have a firewall.

00:29:32.494 --> 00:29:34.160
All right, so the
first thing that we're

00:29:34.160 --> 00:29:35.790
going to do,
automatically, is we're

00:29:35.790 --> 00:29:38.107
going to run GCloud
Firewalls create,

00:29:38.107 --> 00:29:40.440
we're going to open up the
firewall for you on that port

00:29:40.440 --> 00:29:44.530
so that the load balancer
can now talk to your service.

00:29:44.530 --> 00:29:47.350
So now this packet comes
through this firewall--

00:29:47.350 --> 00:29:48.970
or comes through
the load balancer,

00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:50.390
and it enters your GCP network.

00:29:50.390 --> 00:29:52.510
This is the coolest part
Google's network load

00:29:52.510 --> 00:29:55.390
balancer is not a proxy,
it's a packet forwarder.

00:29:55.390 --> 00:29:57.490
So when this packet
comes into your network,

00:29:57.490 --> 00:30:00.520
the destination address is
still the load balancer,

00:30:00.520 --> 00:30:02.050
and the client of
the source address

00:30:02.050 --> 00:30:03.860
is still the original client.

00:30:03.860 --> 00:30:05.440
So you can examine
that IP packet

00:30:05.440 --> 00:30:07.930
and figure out who sent
you this traffic, right.

00:30:07.930 --> 00:30:10.820
That's pretty neat, and
it's really important.

00:30:10.820 --> 00:30:14.220
And I'm going to mess
it up in just a second.

00:30:14.220 --> 00:30:18.350
So this packet arrives at a VM,
and we bring it into this VM,

00:30:18.350 --> 00:30:22.960
the source and destinations
are unchanged from before.

00:30:22.960 --> 00:30:24.120
Now hold on a second.

00:30:24.120 --> 00:30:27.030
I said that it doesn't matter
which VM you land on, right,

00:30:27.030 --> 00:30:31.140
but clearly, one of these VMs
would be a bad choice, right.

00:30:31.140 --> 00:30:33.990
The load balancer, like
all of the cloud load

00:30:33.990 --> 00:30:36.630
balancers as we go through
this container revolution,

00:30:36.630 --> 00:30:39.120
they only know about VMs, right.

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:41.670
VMs don't map one
to one with pods.

00:30:41.670 --> 00:30:44.330
We have more pods then we
do VMs, or we have less pods

00:30:44.330 --> 00:30:46.410
than we do VMs, or we
have the same number

00:30:46.410 --> 00:30:50.130
but they just don't
happen to map correctly.

00:30:50.130 --> 00:30:52.670
We call this the
imbalance problem, right.

00:30:55.190 --> 00:30:56.840
Imagine for a minute
that I actually

00:30:56.840 --> 00:30:59.755
set it up so that it only
sent traffic to these two VMs,

00:30:59.755 --> 00:31:01.880
And we ignored the one that
has no backends, right.

00:31:01.880 --> 00:31:03.630
I'm going to send 50%
of the traffic here,

00:31:03.630 --> 00:31:05.630
I'm going to send 50%
of my traffic here.

00:31:05.630 --> 00:31:08.255
That seems completely reasonable
from the load balancer's point

00:31:08.255 --> 00:31:10.940
of view, since it doesn't know
that there's more VMs on one

00:31:10.940 --> 00:31:12.877
than on the other.

00:31:12.877 --> 00:31:14.460
So at the end, what
you get is one pod

00:31:14.460 --> 00:31:16.410
that has 50% of your
traffic and two pods

00:31:16.410 --> 00:31:17.884
that have 25% of your traffic.

00:31:17.884 --> 00:31:19.800
Whatever monitoring
system you're going to use

00:31:19.800 --> 00:31:21.510
is going to go nuts,
right, it's not

00:31:21.510 --> 00:31:22.560
going to know what
to do with this.

00:31:22.560 --> 00:31:24.768
It's not going to know how
to scale your application,

00:31:24.768 --> 00:31:27.890
and your ops team is
going to hate you.

00:31:27.890 --> 00:31:33.950
I mean, they probably already
do, but they'll hate you more.

00:31:33.950 --> 00:31:35.750
So this is the general
imbalance problem.

00:31:35.750 --> 00:31:38.240
So we set out with
the initial design

00:31:38.240 --> 00:31:40.670
to not trigger the
imbalance problem.

00:31:40.670 --> 00:31:42.260
So how do we avoid
the imbalance?

00:31:42.260 --> 00:31:45.282
We squirt some IP
tables onto it.

00:31:45.282 --> 00:31:48.356
IP tables is the caulk
of networking systems,

00:31:48.356 --> 00:31:49.730
you squirt it in
wherever there's

00:31:49.730 --> 00:31:52.790
a gap to fill in and
smooth things over.

00:31:52.790 --> 00:31:55.430
So this packet
comes into this VM,

00:31:55.430 --> 00:31:57.560
and we're going to
consider which pod am

00:31:57.560 --> 00:31:59.120
I going to send it to, right.

00:31:59.120 --> 00:32:01.310
This is the same path that
we saw before, only now

00:32:01.310 --> 00:32:04.730
it's coming in from
outside, and we

00:32:04.730 --> 00:32:08.210
have to consider all of the pods
across all of the VMs, right.

00:32:08.210 --> 00:32:10.610
So in this case, we're going
to choose pathologically,

00:32:10.610 --> 00:32:13.884
and we're going to pick a
pod on a different VM, right.

00:32:13.884 --> 00:32:15.300
So I'm going to
send this packet--

00:32:15.300 --> 00:32:16.770
first of all, first I'm
doing to do a destination

00:32:16.770 --> 00:32:17.860
NAT, which we saw before.

00:32:17.860 --> 00:32:19.943
This is literally the same
set of IP tables rules.

00:32:19.943 --> 00:32:21.780
So don't think of these
things as additive,

00:32:21.780 --> 00:32:24.170
they are really the
same set of logic.

00:32:24.170 --> 00:32:26.100
I'm going to do my
destination NAT.

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:27.690
I'm going to send
the packet out,

00:32:27.690 --> 00:32:30.290
now note the address
here has changed,

00:32:30.290 --> 00:32:32.550
the definition of says pod2,
but the original is still

00:32:32.550 --> 00:32:34.630
the client, the source IP.

00:32:34.630 --> 00:32:36.540
And I'm going forward
this packet down,

00:32:36.540 --> 00:32:38.610
and it's going to
land at the second VM,

00:32:38.610 --> 00:32:41.610
and just like before it's
going to arrive at this pod.

00:32:41.610 --> 00:32:43.000
We're going to respond.

00:32:43.000 --> 00:32:45.517
We're sending back from
pod2 to our client,

00:32:45.517 --> 00:32:47.100
and because it's
from pod2 the client,

00:32:47.100 --> 00:32:48.810
it's going to go
out to the internet.

00:32:48.810 --> 00:32:51.184
It actually will through that
NAT thing that Michael just

00:32:51.184 --> 00:32:53.680
talked about, but it's going
to be up to the client,

00:32:53.680 --> 00:32:55.741
and it's going to get dropped.

00:32:55.741 --> 00:32:56.740
Why does it get dropped?

00:32:56.740 --> 00:32:59.010
Well, the client sent a
packet to the load balancer,

00:32:59.010 --> 00:33:02.490
it didn't send a packet
to this pod or this VM,

00:33:02.490 --> 00:33:06.360
and so this TCP session isn't
going to complete, right.

00:33:06.360 --> 00:33:09.900
So let's rewind for a minute,
back to when that packet first

00:33:09.900 --> 00:33:11.250
arrived at the first VM.

00:33:11.250 --> 00:33:13.710
We have to do a second NAT,
which is the source NAT.

00:33:13.710 --> 00:33:16.710
We have to change the source
IP to be from this VM.

00:33:16.710 --> 00:33:19.920
I'm forcing the first VM
to be in the traffic path

00:33:19.920 --> 00:33:21.750
in order to achieve
my balancing.

00:33:21.750 --> 00:33:24.630
So now, when you see this
packet fly across the network,

00:33:24.630 --> 00:33:27.780
the source is VM1 and
the destination is pod2.

00:33:27.780 --> 00:33:30.360
All that cool stuff about
keeping the client IP address

00:33:30.360 --> 00:33:32.000
from the network load balancer?

00:33:32.000 --> 00:33:34.310
I just frittered away.

00:33:34.310 --> 00:33:37.310
But just wait, there's more.

00:33:37.310 --> 00:33:39.810
So-- there's more IP tables--

00:33:39.810 --> 00:33:41.690
the packet's going to
arrive at the second VM

00:33:41.690 --> 00:33:43.760
the same way we saw before.

00:33:43.760 --> 00:33:45.380
It's going to be
delivered to the pod,

00:33:45.380 --> 00:33:47.660
it's going to come
back, no problem.

00:33:47.660 --> 00:33:50.870
Conntrack is going
to wake up, realize

00:33:50.870 --> 00:33:54.020
that it has to un-NAT
this packet twice,

00:33:54.020 --> 00:33:58.380
and then it's going
to respond back up.

00:33:58.380 --> 00:33:59.880
The load balancer
will return it,

00:33:59.880 --> 00:34:03.050
and it will merrily go on
its way to your customer.

00:34:03.050 --> 00:34:04.190
Phew.

00:34:04.190 --> 00:34:07.040
That is a little complicated.

00:34:07.040 --> 00:34:11.790
So that imbalance problem,
it's a real problem,

00:34:11.790 --> 00:34:14.900
and we chose to avoid it, right.

00:34:14.900 --> 00:34:17.810
Now, the good news is
that in practice, we're

00:34:17.810 --> 00:34:19.159
really well-balanced.

00:34:19.159 --> 00:34:21.650
The bad news is you have
that extra network hop, which

00:34:21.650 --> 00:34:24.320
can cause some extra
network latency,

00:34:24.320 --> 00:34:27.320
and it hides this client
from the user's backend.

00:34:27.320 --> 00:34:30.260
It turns out,
surprise, users want

00:34:30.260 --> 00:34:32.260
to make this choice
for themselves.

00:34:32.260 --> 00:34:34.880
So--

00:34:34.880 --> 00:34:37.179
MIKE RUBIN: Dun
dun dun, how do you

00:34:37.179 --> 00:34:41.170
deal with this network problem?

00:34:41.170 --> 00:34:43.500
Only local.

00:34:43.500 --> 00:34:46.427
Not just IP tables, we're
going to apply an annotation.

00:34:46.427 --> 00:34:48.760
What are the problems that
we're trying to resolve here?

00:34:48.760 --> 00:34:51.010
Well, sometimes some of
the customers, as Tim

00:34:51.010 --> 00:34:53.889
said, they would rather
risk the imbalance

00:34:53.889 --> 00:34:56.679
or find a way to provision
around the imbalance,

00:34:56.679 --> 00:35:00.434
and they really care about
getting the client IP address.

00:35:00.434 --> 00:35:01.600
They know-- it's a big deal.

00:35:01.600 --> 00:35:03.820
And also they don't want
to deal with the double--

00:35:03.820 --> 00:35:05.420
the latency issues.

00:35:05.420 --> 00:35:09.550
So in Google Containers Engine,
we now support only local.

00:35:09.550 --> 00:35:12.880
You can apply the annotation
only local onto the service,

00:35:12.880 --> 00:35:14.920
and we're going to go
see how that works, too.

00:35:14.920 --> 00:35:19.290
Again, this is all being set
up for you automatically.

00:35:19.290 --> 00:35:21.990
What only local
does is it's going

00:35:21.990 --> 00:35:26.370
to make sure that when you
make that routing selection,

00:35:26.370 --> 00:35:29.470
the VM that you pick
is the local one.

00:35:29.470 --> 00:35:34.040
So as Tim said earlier, the
imbalance here is across VMs,

00:35:34.040 --> 00:35:35.530
were balanced OK.

00:35:35.530 --> 00:35:40.560
We're going to have VM1 versus
VM3 is where the pods live.

00:35:40.560 --> 00:35:43.740
We have a health check to
let us know not to go to VM2.

00:35:43.740 --> 00:35:46.500
But, when you go and check
out the pods below that,

00:35:46.500 --> 00:35:49.230
we can end up with
this imbalance.

00:35:49.230 --> 00:35:51.290
If we have a lot
more pods and nodes,

00:35:51.290 --> 00:35:53.160
this isn't that really
that big an issue.

00:35:53.160 --> 00:35:55.230
It's just sort of noise
for the imbalance,

00:35:55.230 --> 00:35:57.700
if you have 1,000 pods and
let's say only four nodes,

00:35:57.700 --> 00:35:59.340
it doesn't really matter.

00:35:59.340 --> 00:36:02.430
If you have many, many
more nodes and pods,

00:36:02.430 --> 00:36:03.810
well, all of those
pods are going

00:36:03.810 --> 00:36:06.714
to be singletons on
the nodes or the VMs,

00:36:06.714 --> 00:36:08.880
and so you're not going to
have an imbalance either,

00:36:08.880 --> 00:36:10.505
between the health
checks you're always

00:36:10.505 --> 00:36:12.090
going to have a
nice distribution

00:36:12.090 --> 00:36:14.490
at that first level or
the network load balancer

00:36:14.490 --> 00:36:16.860
is doing the balancing to VMs.

00:36:16.860 --> 00:36:19.050
Get into problems when
you have situations

00:36:19.050 --> 00:36:21.600
like this, where the number of
pods and the number of nodes

00:36:21.600 --> 00:36:23.320
are about the same.

00:36:23.320 --> 00:36:25.826
And then the level of--

00:36:25.826 --> 00:36:27.450
like Tim said, you
can have some issues

00:36:27.450 --> 00:36:30.060
where the load that your
pods are dealing with

00:36:30.060 --> 00:36:32.580
are not going to be uniform.

00:36:32.580 --> 00:36:35.510
So with only local on,
the traffic comes in,

00:36:35.510 --> 00:36:37.010
as we said earlier,
the health check

00:36:37.010 --> 00:36:40.870
is not going to consider VM2.

00:36:40.870 --> 00:36:45.210
Source IP address is the client,
destination is load balancer.

00:36:45.210 --> 00:36:48.240
We can choose VM1, VM3,
we're going to pick VM3,

00:36:48.240 --> 00:36:50.070
because it's more
fun and interesting.

00:36:50.070 --> 00:36:54.520
VM1, also fun, maybe
not as much interesting.

00:36:54.520 --> 00:36:58.540
We go to VM3, source
client, destination LB.

00:36:58.540 --> 00:37:02.380
And then when we get on
VM3, we hit the IP tables,

00:37:02.380 --> 00:37:04.570
and remember, the IP
tables could send you

00:37:04.570 --> 00:37:06.850
to anywhere that's
going to have that pod.

00:37:06.850 --> 00:37:08.530
But in this case,
with only local,

00:37:08.530 --> 00:37:11.950
it is only going to send you to
a pod that is local to this VM.

00:37:11.950 --> 00:37:15.250
It's only going to consider
pod2 and pod3, clearly marked

00:37:15.250 --> 00:37:16.240
in green.

00:37:16.240 --> 00:37:18.660
Bright, emerald green.

00:37:18.660 --> 00:37:21.600
It goes to pod2, it easily
could have gone to pod3,

00:37:21.600 --> 00:37:24.100
but we just chose
pod2 for this session.

00:37:24.100 --> 00:37:27.870
[INAUDIBLE] the DNAT, reaches
pod2, and then goes back

00:37:27.870 --> 00:37:30.990
through the same NATing
and Conntracking and IP

00:37:30.990 --> 00:37:33.630
tabling path that we've
discussed over and over again

00:37:33.630 --> 00:37:34.413
during this talk.

00:37:37.870 --> 00:37:40.090
Comes all the way back out
to the node load balancer,

00:37:40.090 --> 00:37:42.310
and then back to the client.

00:37:42.310 --> 00:37:44.250
And as a result,
what's really important

00:37:44.250 --> 00:37:47.470
here is that your pod, the code
that your developers wrote,

00:37:47.470 --> 00:37:50.730
or maybe you wrote, is going to
be able to see the client's IP

00:37:50.730 --> 00:37:51.360
address.

00:37:51.360 --> 00:37:53.640
You're not going to play
these games of not knowing

00:37:53.640 --> 00:37:54.870
where the packet came from.

00:37:54.870 --> 00:37:59.870
You get the normal expected
path that makes the most sense.

00:37:59.870 --> 00:38:00.370
Ingress.

00:38:02.959 --> 00:38:03.750
TIM HOCKIN: Righto.

00:38:03.750 --> 00:38:07.440
So now you all have
internalized exactly how

00:38:07.440 --> 00:38:10.440
Kubernetes works at layer 4.

00:38:10.440 --> 00:38:12.990
So let's talk about
Layer 7, because that's

00:38:12.990 --> 00:38:14.396
way more interesting, right.

00:38:14.396 --> 00:38:16.770
There's been a couple of really
good talks here this week

00:38:16.770 --> 00:38:18.228
about some of the
cool stuff that's

00:38:18.228 --> 00:38:20.310
happening in the Layer 7 space.

00:38:20.310 --> 00:38:23.790
To bring up Layer 7, we
have a different kind

00:38:23.790 --> 00:38:26.730
of resource in Kubernetes,
called an ingress.

00:38:26.730 --> 00:38:31.510
Ingress is built on
top of services, so--

00:38:31.510 --> 00:38:33.160
ingress.

00:38:33.160 --> 00:38:35.410
The first thing you need to
do is change your service

00:38:35.410 --> 00:38:36.550
to a node port.

00:38:36.550 --> 00:38:37.570
Now you could use--

00:38:37.570 --> 00:38:40.540
remember I said earlier you
have cluster IP, node port, load

00:38:40.540 --> 00:38:41.470
balancer.

00:38:41.470 --> 00:38:44.260
You could use load balancer
if you want in L4 also,

00:38:44.260 --> 00:38:46.780
but generally people will
say I only want the L7.

00:38:46.780 --> 00:38:48.670
So I set it up as
type node port.

00:38:48.670 --> 00:38:51.520
What node port does
is it tells Kubernetes

00:38:51.520 --> 00:38:53.950
allocate me a port,
the same port,

00:38:53.950 --> 00:38:57.040
on every VM in my
cluster, and make

00:38:57.040 --> 00:39:00.310
sure that that port
forwards onto my service.

00:39:00.310 --> 00:39:02.920
How do we forward onto
that service, everybody?

00:39:02.920 --> 00:39:04.450
IP tables.

00:39:04.450 --> 00:39:08.170
So-- again, literally it's
the same exact code path

00:39:08.170 --> 00:39:09.440
that we followed before.

00:39:09.440 --> 00:39:12.220
So you declare this thing
as a type node port,

00:39:12.220 --> 00:39:16.760
and then you introduce
an ingress object.

00:39:16.760 --> 00:39:19.420
The ingress object represents
a higher level concept,

00:39:19.420 --> 00:39:22.657
it's an L7 HTTP load balancer.

00:39:22.657 --> 00:39:24.490
And the cool thing about
HTTP load balancers

00:39:24.490 --> 00:39:26.740
is they have the understanding
of what you're actually

00:39:26.740 --> 00:39:28.460
doing at the application level.

00:39:28.460 --> 00:39:31.984
So things like a URL can
map to a different service.

00:39:31.984 --> 00:39:33.400
So in this case,
you can see like,

00:39:33.400 --> 00:39:37.214
the world's simplest ingress
has a /customers URL,

00:39:37.214 --> 00:39:39.880
and that customer's URL is going
to go to our customers backend,

00:39:39.880 --> 00:39:43.120
and the /products URL is going
to go to our products backend,

00:39:43.120 --> 00:39:43.720
right.

00:39:43.720 --> 00:39:45.940
Each of these two backends
are different services,

00:39:45.940 --> 00:39:49.720
which are backed
by a set of pods,

00:39:49.720 --> 00:39:51.850
through this selector
mechanism that we've been

00:39:51.850 --> 00:39:52.975
seeing over and over again.

00:39:55.350 --> 00:39:59.010
So you can see here, sorry,
I just highlighted that.

00:39:59.010 --> 00:40:03.000
So, again, just like a
service, when you do this,

00:40:03.000 --> 00:40:04.350
the controller wakes up.

00:40:04.350 --> 00:40:06.690
The controller goes out to
Google Cloud and it programs

00:40:06.690 --> 00:40:08.910
the HTTP load balancer.

00:40:08.910 --> 00:40:11.820
Again, if anybody's familiar
with the Google Cloud API,

00:40:11.820 --> 00:40:15.420
the HTTP load balancer is
actually about eight resources

00:40:15.420 --> 00:40:16.530
to the Google Cloud API.

00:40:16.530 --> 00:40:19.071
It's sort of complicated to set
up because it's so incredibly

00:40:19.071 --> 00:40:19.839
powerful.

00:40:19.839 --> 00:40:21.630
We take that for you,
we do it all for you,

00:40:21.630 --> 00:40:24.570
we configure the instance
groups and the backends,

00:40:24.570 --> 00:40:27.306
and the forwarding rules
and the target proxies.

00:40:27.306 --> 00:40:28.680
We set it all up,
and in the end,

00:40:28.680 --> 00:40:30.061
we point your IP address here.

00:40:30.061 --> 00:40:31.560
Again, you can take
this IP address,

00:40:31.560 --> 00:40:35.230
you can put it in your
DNS, and off to the races.

00:40:35.230 --> 00:40:39.705
So let's take a look
at some traffic again.

00:40:39.705 --> 00:40:41.080
So we've got a
cluster, we've got

00:40:41.080 --> 00:40:43.930
our blue and green services,
our customers and our products.

00:40:43.930 --> 00:40:48.450
These ports exist on every node,
so when you create that ser--

00:40:48.450 --> 00:40:52.120
the ingress object, we're going
to go off and create the Google

00:40:52.120 --> 00:40:54.640
Cloud Load Balancer.

00:40:54.640 --> 00:40:56.410
Google Cloud Load
Balancer, as before, we

00:40:56.410 --> 00:41:01.030
teach it about all three VMs
through the instance group

00:41:01.030 --> 00:41:03.970
mechanism, and we start
taking our traffic.

00:41:03.970 --> 00:41:08.470
So, our client sends
something to our load balancer

00:41:08.470 --> 00:41:12.380
on the /products path,
that's the green one.

00:41:12.380 --> 00:41:14.249
We're going to take
the packet through,

00:41:14.249 --> 00:41:16.040
and again, the load
balancer has to choose,

00:41:16.040 --> 00:41:18.412
which VM am I going
to send this to.

00:41:18.412 --> 00:41:20.120
It's going to look at
all the information

00:41:20.120 --> 00:41:23.390
that it has available to it,
and it's going to choose a VM.

00:41:23.390 --> 00:41:25.377
In this case it's
going to choose VM3.

00:41:25.377 --> 00:41:27.460
Now I said that we're
looking at the green service

00:41:27.460 --> 00:41:30.830
and I chose three to
sort of the pessimal.

00:41:30.830 --> 00:41:33.050
It's going to send
this traffic off

00:41:33.050 --> 00:41:36.740
to the VM now take note
here of the IP addresses

00:41:36.740 --> 00:41:39.166
the source here is
the load balancer.

00:41:39.166 --> 00:41:41.540
But it's not actually the IP
address of the load balancer

00:41:41.540 --> 00:41:45.200
that you had, it's sort of
a Google internal address

00:41:45.200 --> 00:41:47.240
that we use for all of
our load balancing pool.

00:41:47.240 --> 00:41:49.430
We've published
docs on this range

00:41:49.430 --> 00:41:50.870
that we use for
a load balancers.

00:41:50.870 --> 00:41:54.640
This is because our HTTP load
balancer is a full proxy,

00:41:54.640 --> 00:41:56.677
right, it will terminate
your TCP session.

00:41:56.677 --> 00:41:58.760
So you could do things
like SSL, where you give us

00:41:58.760 --> 00:42:01.340
your certificate, we'll
terminate the SSL,

00:42:01.340 --> 00:42:03.860
and we'll pass the
packets onto the back.

00:42:03.860 --> 00:42:06.272
What it means is you lose
that client IP again,

00:42:06.272 --> 00:42:08.480
but you don't actually lose
it, because this is HTTP,

00:42:08.480 --> 00:42:11.540
and HTTP is so cool because
it's got things like headers.

00:42:11.540 --> 00:42:13.670
So the client IP
address will be stuck

00:42:13.670 --> 00:42:16.340
in that X forwarded for
header, which you can then

00:42:16.340 --> 00:42:19.670
use if any piece of your
stack understands HTTP,

00:42:19.670 --> 00:42:22.830
you can pull that out
and log that correctly.

00:42:22.830 --> 00:42:25.952
So now the packet
is destined for VM3.

00:42:25.952 --> 00:42:28.160
So we've changed both the
source and the destination.

00:42:28.160 --> 00:42:30.310
If we go back, you
see the packet here

00:42:30.310 --> 00:42:36.540
is client to load balancer,
here it's load balancer to VM.

00:42:36.540 --> 00:42:38.770
This packet is going to
arrive at the greenport.

00:42:38.770 --> 00:42:40.270
And this is
important, because we

00:42:40.270 --> 00:42:43.270
use the receipt
at that greenport

00:42:43.270 --> 00:42:45.610
as a trigger to say,
hey, I know what to do

00:42:45.610 --> 00:42:47.710
with this, this a service.

00:42:47.710 --> 00:42:49.230
And I'm going to send it--

00:42:49.230 --> 00:42:52.270
oh shoot, I'm going to
send it to IP tables again.

00:42:52.270 --> 00:42:54.730
Same thing we've
seen six times now.

00:42:54.730 --> 00:42:56.890
IP tables is going
to choose a pod,

00:42:56.890 --> 00:43:00.802
it's going to do that NAT
that we saw before, it's

00:43:00.802 --> 00:43:02.260
going to bounce
out of the machine,

00:43:02.260 --> 00:43:05.094
it's going to fly
over our network,

00:43:05.094 --> 00:43:07.260
we're going to receive it,
no tricks this time, it's

00:43:07.260 --> 00:43:12.520
actually going to work, and
it's going to come back.

00:43:12.520 --> 00:43:14.200
Now we've received
it in IP tables.

00:43:14.200 --> 00:43:20.530
We do the opposite of that
contract that we saw before,

00:43:20.530 --> 00:43:25.100
and we send the packet back on,
and we have a happy customer.

00:43:25.100 --> 00:43:28.782
So it's even more
complicated than L4,

00:43:28.782 --> 00:43:30.490
and it suffers some
of the same problems.

00:43:30.490 --> 00:43:32.980
You've got this double
hop, still, right.

00:43:32.980 --> 00:43:35.440
So, it turns out,
customers still

00:43:35.440 --> 00:43:38.650
want to make this
decision for themselves.

00:43:38.650 --> 00:43:42.340
MIKE RUBIN: So we don't have to
worry about losing the client's

00:43:42.340 --> 00:43:44.340
IP address, but
as Tim just said,

00:43:44.340 --> 00:43:46.930
the double hop is
still a problem

00:43:46.930 --> 00:43:49.420
and it adds that complexity.

00:43:49.420 --> 00:43:50.830
Everyone here can
see this slide,

00:43:50.830 --> 00:43:53.230
it looks very similar to
the other only local side

00:43:53.230 --> 00:43:54.550
for a service.

00:43:54.550 --> 00:43:57.610
You can have inside of ingress
these sort of subservices based

00:43:57.610 --> 00:43:59.950
on the path or the
annotations that you want,

00:43:59.950 --> 00:44:02.595
and you can specify
only local again.

00:44:02.595 --> 00:44:04.720
You can configure the
subservice inside the ingress

00:44:04.720 --> 00:44:08.420
is only local, and you risk
the imbalance as before,

00:44:08.420 --> 00:44:11.980
which can usually be avoided by
how you choose to apportion out

00:44:11.980 --> 00:44:16.570
your nodes and pods, and
you remove the second hop.

00:44:16.570 --> 00:44:18.760
So there's a lot more
that we could discuss.

00:44:18.760 --> 00:44:21.370
I mean, I can just
read from the slide.

00:44:21.370 --> 00:44:23.440
Pod liveness probe,
graceful termination,

00:44:23.440 --> 00:44:26.230
cloud health checks, firewalls,
and so on and so forth.

00:44:26.230 --> 00:44:27.915
But networking is really deep.

00:44:27.915 --> 00:44:30.040
The functionality and
features that people want out

00:44:30.040 --> 00:44:32.770
of Google Container Engine
for networking, the list

00:44:32.770 --> 00:44:35.530
goes on and on and
on and on and on.

00:44:35.530 --> 00:44:38.410
Instead of talking about
each one of these subtopics,

00:44:38.410 --> 00:44:40.600
we really wanted to make
sure that people understood

00:44:40.600 --> 00:44:44.230
the routing basics, both in
and out of the inter cluster--

00:44:44.230 --> 00:44:46.131
intercluster ingress and egress.

00:44:46.131 --> 00:44:48.130
And that's sort of what
we're hoping to do here.

00:44:48.130 --> 00:44:50.170
I know that this got
a little repetitious,

00:44:50.170 --> 00:44:52.752
but it's because we build and
don't waste the functionality

00:44:52.752 --> 00:44:55.210
and infrastructure that we have
instead of Google Container

00:44:55.210 --> 00:44:55.825
Engine.

00:44:55.825 --> 00:44:57.700
TIM HOCKIN: Can I add--

00:44:57.700 --> 00:44:59.560
I've spent the week
here working downstairs

00:44:59.560 --> 00:45:01.990
at the Meet the Experts
table, and I've spoken to,

00:45:01.990 --> 00:45:03.880
I know, some of you.

00:45:03.880 --> 00:45:06.250
I have wanted to
pull these slides out

00:45:06.250 --> 00:45:08.350
at least a dozen times
over the last two days

00:45:08.350 --> 00:45:10.030
to explain concepts.

00:45:10.030 --> 00:45:12.490
And so I thought it was
really important that we

00:45:12.490 --> 00:45:14.350
get this information out there.

00:45:14.350 --> 00:45:16.960
I get the sense from a lot of
people that this is complicated

00:45:16.960 --> 00:45:18.437
and they don't understand it.

00:45:18.437 --> 00:45:20.770
MIKE RUBIN: It's an interesting
talk, because by the end

00:45:20.770 --> 00:45:24.280
you get a lot of OK, we've
got it, we've got it,

00:45:24.280 --> 00:45:26.470
you reinforced it, but
then before the talk,

00:45:26.470 --> 00:45:28.030
a lot of people
want to know exactly

00:45:28.030 --> 00:45:29.380
about this sort of topic.

00:45:29.380 --> 00:45:32.140
And so even instead of Google,
when we wrote this talk,

00:45:32.140 --> 00:45:35.190
it's gotten a lot of interest.

00:45:35.190 --> 00:45:36.421
Watch this space.

00:45:36.421 --> 00:45:38.670
Google Container Engine is
definitely a moving target.

00:45:38.670 --> 00:45:40.710
There's the open source
components and the open source

00:45:40.710 --> 00:45:42.360
architecture, which
has been worked on

00:45:42.360 --> 00:45:45.660
by companies and engineers
all over the world.

00:45:45.660 --> 00:45:47.850
And, you know, lots
of rearchitecture,

00:45:47.850 --> 00:45:52.110
and lots of just bug fixes and
elegant and simplifications

00:45:52.110 --> 00:45:56.190
and development work is being
undergone, even as we speak.

00:45:56.190 --> 00:45:58.860
Even Google, we're working on
the Google Container Engine

00:45:58.860 --> 00:46:02.580
part as we host it and integrate
it with the GCP environment.

00:46:02.580 --> 00:46:05.670
So Next '18, it's going
to look different.

00:46:05.670 --> 00:46:07.590
It will be looking
maybe faster, maybe

00:46:07.590 --> 00:46:10.410
less IP tables, for
some of the problems

00:46:10.410 --> 00:46:12.480
that we just elaborated on.

00:46:12.480 --> 00:46:15.450
Probably more IP tables for
new functionality and new

00:46:15.450 --> 00:46:18.784
features, and different ways
to approach these problems.

00:46:18.784 --> 00:46:20.700
So there will definitely
be more ins and outs,

00:46:20.700 --> 00:46:23.400
and we hope that you come
back to learn more about it

00:46:23.400 --> 00:46:23.944
